# AntiQuickPad architecture

T-012 preserves the resident Classic UI while separating stored state and text rules from rendering. Both product projects target net10.0; Core has no package references or Avalonia types. The small Core.Smoke console is a disposable capability check.

```text
App / Program (composition and CLI entry points)
    constructs Core NotesSession + SettingsStore -> MainViewModel
    constructs platform services -> chosen IShellView

Views/Classic, Design (IEditorHost), Editor, Carousel
                         |
                         v
ViewModels: MainViewModel -> NoteViewModel / StatusViewModel
                         |
                         v
AntiQuickPad.Core: NotesSession, Note, SettingsStore, Text

Resident shell -> Shell interfaces -> Avalonia / Windows implementations
Measurement / smoke harnesses exercise these boundaries independently.
```

Dependency rule: **Views -> ViewModels -> Core; nothing points up.** Core never references UI, dispatcher, window, editor, or platform APIs. ViewModels use plain INPC and ICommand helpers and never reference Avalonia. Core DTOs are used by the editor adapter for text edit plans; the adapter calls ViewModel methods, which call Core. App alone constructs dependencies by hand. There is no service locator or dependency injection package.

| Feature | Owner |
| --- | --- |
| Ordered Markdown notes, IDs, current selection, caret, keep-one-note deletion | Core/NotesSession and Note |
| Atomic Markdown/manifest writes; explicit Save/Flush and save-state events | Core/NotesSession |
| Pin, normal window bounds, shell/look identifiers, atomic settings replacement | Core/SettingsStore and WindowSettings |
| Markdown/Python classification, heading/fence fold spans | Core/Text/MarkdownScanner |
| Checkbox toggle, list continuation/exit, Tab/outdent and ordered-list numbering | Core/Text/TextOperations returns granular edit plans |
| Notes projection, commands, current index/count, status/error, pin state, settings adapter | ViewModels/MainViewModel, NoteViewModel, StatusViewModel |
| Existing toolbar/status layout, editor hosting, focus cue, Close=hide, lifecycle and 350 ms debounce | Views/Classic/ClassicShellWindow |
| Editor creation, undo groups, colors/fonts, fold manager, checkboxes/strike/indent drawing, scroll offsets | Editor, configured by Look (DesignSpec supplies fixture looks) |
| Wheel burst gate, drag capture/snap, preview and 220 ms slide | Carousel; selection requests go to MainViewModel |
| Global hotkey, tray, singleton signals and platform window operations | Shell interfaces and their concrete implementations |
| Isolated render-design/probe-backdrop fixtures | Design; shares IEditorHost and EditorFactory without opening resident notes |
| --measure, --selftest and isolated Core smoke | Measurement, ClassicShellWindow.Smoke, Core.Smoke |

Navigation has explicit before/after events: the shell cancels animation, captures editor text/caret, saves and remembers scroll before the VM changes selection. A failed save cancels navigation. After a change the shell binds the selected note, restores scroll, clears the editor undo stack, saves the new selection and restores focus. Scroll vectors, editor documents, animation and focus never enter Core. Core events are synchronous on the caller thread; the UI invokes operations on its dispatcher thread. Save failures remain visible until a successful save.

IShellView exposes the resident Window host, editor, MainViewModel and show/hide/quit hooks. Its smaller IEditorHost contract is also implemented by the isolated design window; the fixture host deliberately has no resident ViewModel or services. Classic's smoke checks are kept in a separate partial file. Platform factories are constructor-injected through ShellServices. ISingleInstanceService is owned by Program; the command-only --quit path opens the existing channel and returns before Avalonia startup.

Existing notes.json, .md files and settings.json remain readable. Optional ShellId/LookId settings default to classic/Ink for legacy files; saves preserve loaded IDs. Only Classic is available in T-012, so unknown/future IDs fall back to Classic until their constructors are registered. The settings store contains identifiers, never renderer objects.

## How to add a new shell

1. Add Views/Quiet or Views/Card and implement IShellView with a constructor taking the existing MainViewModel and ShellServices; host one EditorFactory.Create(look, viewModel) editor. Reuse the resident lifecycle patterns from Classic.
2. Build the visual tree and bind New/Delete/Previous/Next/Pin controls to existing VM commands/properties. Observe Status and CountText, and keep all note/store access behind the VM.
3. Connect navigation capture/rebind hooks, CarouselController, per-note scroll restoration and platform show/hide/pin/bounds services. Keep focus, debounce, Close=hide and save-failure behavior consistent with Classic.
4. Add the constructor to App's ShellId switch and provide its Look/DesignSpec entirely in the UI project. Persist its ID through the settings adapter. Core and ViewModels need no changes to switch shells.
5. Run the offline Release build, standalone Core smoke and --selftest; render the shell fixture at both scales, then run --measure and physical hotkey/tray/editor/carousel/DPI checks on the desktop before selecting it by default.

## Local verification

From src/AntiQuickPad: run `dotnet bin/Release/net10.0/AntiQuickPad.dll --quit`, then `$env:AVALONIA_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT='1'; dotnet build -c Release --no-restore -p:UseSharedCompilation=false` and `dotnet bin/Release/net10.0/AntiQuickPad.dll --selftest`.

From the repository root: build `src/AntiQuickPad.Core.Smoke` with the same Release/no-restore flags and run `dotnet src/AntiQuickPad.Core.Smoke/bin/Release/net10.0/AntiQuickPad.Core.Smoke.dll`. Its only project reference is Core; it checks both assembly references and actually loaded assemblies as well as persistence and legacy/settings round trips. New project assets can be generated with `dotnet restore <project> --source src` (local directory plus installed package cache; no network feed).

Scripted focus checks establish internal show-to-focus only. Physical hotkey latency, presentation, normal-desktop resource use and subjective rendering remain desktop measurements.
# M6 — three ways to make room for a thought

These are **real Avalonia 12.1.4 / AvaloniaEdit 12.0.0 renders**, generated locally from `src/AntiQuickPad/Design`. Each direction uses the production `EditorFactory.Create(Look)`: the existing Markdown/Python colorizer, code-panel, checkbox, active indent-guide and strike renderers, document scanner and folding manager. The new margin replaces stock fold boxes with clickable chevrons. A design-only renderer adds the active line. No browser, raster mockups, downloads or simulated backdrop.

All frames are 960 × 800 client DIPs, rendered by `RenderTargetBitmap` at 1× and 1.5× vector scale (960 × 800 and 1440 × 1200 pixels). The standard Windows title bar, DWM shadow and OS corners are outside the captured client visual tree. **The live window remains opaque and uses standard decorations.** Internal Card corners/shadows are real Avalonia Borders; they do not depend on transparent HWND corners.

The fixed main fixture contains H1/H2, prose, bullets, nested completed/open tasks, a Python fence, an indented caret at line 13 / column 21, and a struck line. `main` uses subdued bars; `bars` shows full-strength bars with the More control highlighted. `menu` opens an in-tree menu surface so it is included in RTB. `carousel` is a deterministic intermediate translation of two full-width real editor trees, rather than a photograph of a running animation. `empty` loads an actually empty editor and adds a hint overlay. A Viewbox applies the vector scale before RTB rasterization at 96 DPI; direct 144-DPI RTB capture produced doubled nested text and missing menu contents on this build and was rejected. The 1.5× frames test raster scale, not physical monitor switching or Windows text-size accessibility settings.

The design host is deliberately separate from resident notes, autosave, tray and global hotkey registration. It never opens the user's notes. Pin, minimize, hide and More are real controls; the New/Delete/navigation and menu command **layouts** are review candidates, not a second notes application. Settings and folder commands are proposed affordances. Selecting a design still requires integrating its shell with `MainWindow` and `CarouselController`. The normal entry point and default appearance are unchanged.

## A · Quiet

A generous writing surface with one clear left edge and almost no furniture.  
Small monochrome controls sit at the edges; the page itself has no container.  
For the person who summons the pad, writes a thought, and leaves.

![Quiet — main](quiet/main@1x.png)

| State | 1× | 1.5× |
|---|---|---|
| Main | [PNG](quiet/main@1x.png) | [PNG](quiet/main@1.5x.png) |
| Bars / More hover | [PNG](quiet/bars@1x.png) | [PNG](quiet/bars@1.5x.png) |
| More menu | [PNG](quiet/menu@1x.png) | [PNG](quiet/menu@1.5x.png) |
| Mid-slide | [PNG](quiet/carousel@1x.png) | [PNG](quiet/carousel@1.5x.png) |
| Empty | [PNG](quiet/empty@1x.png) | [PNG](quiet/empty@1.5x.png) |

**Changes vs today.** Replaces the row of labeled rectangular buttons and full-width status box with a unified, quiet top toolbar and a baseline of navigation/save information. New and More live at top-right; Previous/Next and `3 of 8` live at bottom-left; delete is kept at the opposite edge. Pin, minimize and hide retain a consistent trailing order. A muted collection eyebrow provides orientation without another panel. Future hover-reveal changes only bar opacity/hit testing, keeping the writing surface still; keyboard focus must also reveal the bars.

**Design details.** Segoe UI Variable Text, 17 DIP body / 30 H1 / 22 H2; 4/8/12/24 spacing rhythm, roughly 100 DIP writing inset. Segoe Fluent Icons at 15 DIP in 32 × 30 controls (compact desktop targets). No line numbers; 16 DIP chevron lane. The sage active row and active indent guide are visible without turning into another box. Real 14 DIP rounded checkboxes, 6 DIP rounded code background, and a narrow muted scrollbar. Only the menu/control hover has an internal radius; no fake rounded outer window or painted exterior shadow. Save state stays centered and page count remains textual.

**Motion proposal — not implemented.**

| Feedback | Proposed behavior | Difficulty / reason |
|---|---|---|
| F5 show/hide | Keep the resident window alive; immediate focus, then optional short opacity settle. Respect reduced motion. Use native minimize/restore where appropriate. | **Medium**. Avalonia Show/Hide does not by itself establish the requested Windows shell animation; exact native hide behavior needs a Win32 spike. Do not delay focus for an animation. |
| F21 scroll | 100–140 ms eased interpolation of the real vertical ScrollViewer offset; rapid wheel input retargets the destination. | **Medium**. `EditorViewport` already reaches the real viewer; clamp to extent and preserve horizontal carousel routing. |
| F22 caret | A short position interpolation, capped at one row on typing; jump immediately for large navigation and IME composition. | **Hard**. Needs a custom caret visual while the logical caret/selection/IME stay authoritative. |
| F23 folds | Brief fade/clip of the collapsing section, then commit the real folded layout. | **Hard**. AvaloniaEdit changes visual lines immediately; requires a temporary snapshot/overlay and careful scroll anchoring. |

**Limits hit.** Natural font metrics control leading. Code text remains flush to the code-panel left edge; the design does not claim inner padding. Checkbox rows use the existing baseline-preserving renderer. Custom chevrons escape the brush-only stock margin limit. Low-strength idle chrome needs the user's contrast judgment on their monitor.

**Estimated effort after selection:** 2–3 working days for shell/editor styling, menu command wiring, hover/focus reveal and DPI checks; motion work is additional (shared estimate below).

## B · Card

A note has a physical place in a small collection.  
Two neighbouring edges, a lifted page, and a floating pager make the carousel legible.  
For the person who thinks in several short notes at once.

![Card — main](card/main@1x.png)

| State | 1× | 1.5× |
|---|---|---|
| Main | [PNG](card/main@1x.png) | [PNG](card/main@1.5x.png) |
| Bars / More hover | [PNG](card/bars@1x.png) | [PNG](card/bars@1.5x.png) |
| More menu | [PNG](card/menu@1x.png) | [PNG](card/menu@1.5x.png) |
| Mid-slide | [PNG](card/carousel@1x.png) | [PNG](card/carousel@1.5x.png) |
| Empty | [PNG](card/empty@1x.png) | [PNG](card/empty@1.5x.png) |

**Changes vs today.** Moves the note into an 816 DIP-wide card with real inset content and visible neighbouring edges, rather than filling a rectangular editor viewport. The top action cluster and bottom pager float on the canvas, independent of the page; count and saved state balance the lower corners. Page dots supplement the exact count. The bars can later reveal on edge hover/focus without resizing cards. In the empty state, `9 of 9` moves the selected dot to the collection's last visible position; a production collection larger than eight needs a sliding dot range or compressed indicator.

**Design details.** Segoe UI Variable Text, 16 DIP body / 30 H1 / 22 H2. An 8 DIP rhythm with 44 DIP card padding and 16 DIP corner radius. The opaque pale card has a fine boundary and a small real BoxShadow against a blue-grey canvas. Floating controls use 12 DIP corners. Periwinkle active row, softly rounded 15 DIP checkboxes, subdued chevrons without line numbers, and a narrow scrollbar. A pill marks the active page among dots. Carousel frames translate two full-size editor cards, keeping line-wrap width constant.

**Motion proposal — not implemented.**

| Feedback | Proposed behavior | Difficulty / reason |
|---|---|---|
| F5 show/hide | Show/focus immediately; a very small whole-surface settle, not a zoom that reflows text. Native minimize/restore when available. | **Medium**. Needs compositor measurement and reduced-motion path; exact Windows show/hide remains a separate native investigation. |
| F21 scroll | Eased vertical note scrolling while card position stays fixed. Cancel interpolation on text selection/scrollbar drag. | **Medium**. Same ScrollViewer technique; must distinguish vertical scrolling from page gestures. |
| F22 caret | Short interpolation only within the active card; snap on a page change. | **Hard**. Native caret replacement plus coordinate transforms during a slide; never animate IME anchors. |
| F23 folds | A short clipped content transition inside the card; the card bounds and floating toolbar remain stable. | **Hard**. Snapshot plus real folded layout, scroll anchoring, and no stale interactive checkbox hit targets. |

**Limits hit.** Natural line height and flush-left code text remain. A visual pass found the last fixture line clipped; the final card uses smaller body type and revised vertical padding to fit it. The neighbouring slivers are static edge surfaces in resting frames; mid-slide uses a second real editor. The OS shadow is not present in RTB. More layers and shadows mean this direction needs the most GPU/gesture measurement before adoption.

**Estimated effort after selection:** 4–6 working days including production carousel/card geometry, edge peeks, collection-aware dots, toolbar command wiring, pointer/focus reveal and DPI checks; motion additional.

## C · Pro

An orderly tool for people who navigate with the keyboard.  
A compact toolbar, numbered gutter and useful status line establish a working rhythm.  
For longer scratch notes, code fragments and frequent movement between pages.

![Pro — main](pro/main@1x.png)

| State | 1× | 1.5× |
|---|---|---|
| Main | [PNG](pro/main@1x.png) | [PNG](pro/main@1.5x.png) |
| Bars / More hover | [PNG](pro/bars@1x.png) | [PNG](pro/bars@1.5x.png) |
| More menu | [PNG](pro/menu@1x.png) | [PNG](pro/menu@1.5x.png) |
| Mid-slide | [PNG](pro/carousel@1x.png) | [PNG](pro/carousel@1.5x.png) |
| Empty | [PNG](pro/empty@1x.png) | [PNG](pro/empty@1.5x.png) |

**Changes vs today.** A 48 DIP toolbar and compact collection strip replace the wrapping button row. A narrow navigation rail separates collection tools from note actions. A 30 DIP bottom status strip contains line/column, word count, Markdown/UTF-8 and save state; shortcut hints sit just above it. The status changes to line 1 / column 1 and 0 words for the genuinely empty document. Top and bottom live in overlay slots so future hover-reveal can preserve viewport geometry. The extra rail and status information make this intentionally denser than the other two directions.

**Design details.** Consolas body 15 DIP / H1 25 / H2 20, paired with Segoe UI Variable Text for controls. 4/8/12/16 spacing, line numbers and a 16 DIP custom chevron lane. Broad slate active row, brighter active indent guide, 13 DIP checkboxes with 3 DIP radius, a 6 DIP code-panel radius, and narrow muted scrollbar. Hairline separators define toolbar, gutter and status rather than separate floating boxes. Page count is compact `03 / 08`. Menu corners are 10 DIP; the main surface relies on the opaque standard window's real OS treatment.

**Motion proposal — not implemented.**

| Feedback | Proposed behavior | Difficulty / reason |
|---|---|---|
| F5 show/hide | Immediate focus/visibility; native minimize/restore; optional very short opacity settle only. | **Medium**. Preserve resident behavior and measure focus before decoration; exact shell hide animation requires a native probe. |
| F21 scroll | Short, restrained 80–100 ms wheel interpolation; PageUp/PageDown and scrollbar dragging stay direct. | **Medium**. Existing ScrollViewer access, but wheel ownership and interrupted animation need care. |
| F22 caret | A short same-line interpolation; snap for multicaret/selection, jumps, page changes and IME. | **Hard**. Custom visual must not change the logical editor caret or accessibility/IME reporting. |
| F23 folds | Prefer a fast fade with stable gutter markers; animate content height only after a successful spike. | **Hard**. AvaloniaEdit has immediate folding and variable-height wrapped visual lines. A height animation is not an exposed switch. |

**Limits hit.** Dense natural font metrics are intentional; no custom leading. Code-panel inner padding is still unavailable. The default Cascadia Mono choice appeared too heavy during visual QA; the final candidate uses Consolas with an explicit regular base typeface before the production Markdown transformer. The rail/search and shortcut chips are proposed shell affordances; this task does not implement a search feature. Exact line/word state is fixture-driven here and must be bound to the live document on integration.

**Estimated effort after selection:** 3–4 working days for toolbar/status/rail styling and live bindings, menu wiring, hover/focus reveal and DPI checks; search functionality is outside this estimate; motion additional.

## Shared motion budget and boundaries

Engineering estimates, not measured delivery promises: 1–2 days for an F5 native-window spike and reversible treatment, 1–2 days for F21 wheel interpolation, 2–4 days for F22 custom caret, 3–5 days for an F23 folding spike. Exact macOS-style leading or exact OS shell animation through only stock AvaloniaEdit properties is **not feasible**; custom work or a less ambitious behavior is required. Animated folding may be rejected after the spike. Do not hold input/focus behind animation; reduced motion disables decorative movement. Horizontal carousel motion already exists in the product; these stills do not validate its feel or performance in the new layouts.

## F25 · standard-window backdrop probe

Run from `src/AntiQuickPad`:

```powershell
dotnet bin/Release/net10.0/AntiQuickPad.dll --probe-backdrop mica
# Other values: none, acrylic, blur. Close the independent probe to exit.
# Optional unattended capability recording:
dotnet bin/Release/net10.0/AntiQuickPad.dll --probe-backdrop mica --report ../../docs/design/m6/probe-mica.txt --close-after-ms 400
```

Each probe opens a **real standard-decorated window** with exactly the requested `TransparencyLevelHint`, a #99171D25 semitransparent client background and an opaque fallback. It waits 800 ms after Opened, then reports `ActualTransparencyLevel` to the window, stdout and the optional report file. `--close-after-ms` counts from that report; omitting it leaves the window open for human inspection. It registers no hotkey/tray and never opens the notes store.

Observed locally on OS API string `Microsoft Windows 10.0.26340`, Avalonia assembly **12.1.4.0**:

| Input | Hint | ActualTransparencyLevel | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| none | None | None | [report](probe-none.txt) |
| mica | Mica | Mica | [report](probe-mica.txt) |
| acrylic | AcrylicBlur | AcrylicBlur | [report](probe-acrylic.txt) |
| blur | Blur | Transparent | [report](probe-blur.txt) |

**Blur was not granted**: Avalonia reports Transparent instead. Mica/Acrylic are reported levels, not proof that the resulting desktop effect is attractive, stable or identical to the intended material. RTB cannot capture a compositor backdrop. The manager/user still needs to inspect each live mode over light/dark content, inactive/focused, moving/resizing and across DPI changes. The OS version's `10.0` prefix is the Windows API's version string, not a claim that this is a Windows 10 desktop.

## Validation

Final Release build: **0 warnings / 0 errors** ([log](build.log)). Final `--selftest`: **exit 0** ([log](selftest.log)); scripted resident show-to-focus **2.667 / 1.605 / 1.089 ms**. All 30 final PNGs were individually viewed and passed dimension checks ([manifest](render-manifest.json)). One measurement-child timeout was recovered with graceful quit and an isolated unchanged-binary retry; retained [failure log](selftest-timeout.log). No physical hotkey-to-paint or future motion performance is claimed.

## Reproduce and validate

```powershell
# From src/AntiQuickPad; manager already restored dependencies.
dotnet bin/Release/net10.0/AntiQuickPad.dll --quit
$env:AVALONIA_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT='1'
dotnet build -c Release --no-restore -p:UseSharedCompilation=false
foreach ($direction in 'quiet','card','pro') {
    dotnet bin/Release/net10.0/AntiQuickPad.dll --render-design $direction "../../docs/design/m6/$direction"
}
dotnet bin/Release/net10.0/AntiQuickPad.dll --selftest
```

Render flags bypass the normal singleton and data paths, use a fixed fixture, render five states at both scales, then gracefully close. Neither restore nor network access is required. The selftest exercises the production resident path; it does not measure future motion or a selected design integrated into that resident path. See [T-011 report](../../../.coord/reports/T-011.md) for final checks and user-eye items.

## Comparison

| | Quiet | Card | Pro |
|---|---|---|---|
| Main organizing idea | One writing surface | A spatial collection | A keyboard workspace |
| Top/bottom bars | Minimal edge toolbar + quiet footer | Floating tool/pager capsules | Compact toolbar + useful status |
| Page position | Text count + arrows | Peeks + dots + exact count | Compact fraction + collection strip |
| Editor | Airy proportional text, no numbers | Padded paper card, no numbers | Dense monospace, numbered gutter |
| Borders/corners | Almost invisible; OS exterior | Internal radius/elevation; OS exterior | Hairline divisions; OS exterior |
| Strongest advantage | Least visual competition | Clearest carousel identity | Most information in reach |
| Main tradeoff | Muted idle controls | Less writing area, more layers | More persistent visual structure |
| Selection estimate, excluding motion | 2–3 days | 4–6 days | 3–4 days |
| First human check | Readability and restraint | Card spacing and page-switch feel | Body weight, density, status usefulness |




# T-011 — M6 frontend directions

Status: COMPLETE — all requested design/probe artifacts and final validation delivered. User selection and live visual checks remain.

## Delivered

- Three structurally different, real Avalonia directions: **Quiet** (unified writing surface), **Card** (elevated note/peeks/floating pager), **Pro** (compact toolbar/numbered gutter/status/command hints).
- `src/AntiQuickPad/Design/DesignEntry.cs`, `DesignSpec.cs`, `DesignWindow.cs`, `ChevronMargin.cs`. Only two small normal-source hooks: Program dispatches the design/probe flags; App creates the isolated design or probe window. Normal resident creation is unchanged.
- `--render-design <quiet|card|pro> <outdir>` uses real production EditorFactory, Markdown/Python transformer, code background, checkbox/guide/strike renderers and folding manager; adds a regular base-typeface transformer, active-line renderer and clickable custom chevron margin in the design host.
- 30 PNGs: three directions × main/bars/menu/carousel/empty × 1×/1.5×. **Viewed every final PNG individually** after repairs. Correct 960×800 / 1440×1200 dimensions and SHA256 recorded in `docs/design/m6/render-manifest.json`.
- `docs/design/m6/README.md`: every PNG linked, three-line pitches, layout/type/icon/gutter/code/checkbox/scrollbar details, differences vs today, per-direction motion proposals/difficulties, known limits, effort estimates, reproduction/probe instructions and final comparison table.
- `--probe-backdrop <none|mica|acrylic|blur>` opens an independent real standard-chrome window with semitransparent background. Optional `--report path` and `--close-after-ms n` allow graceful capability recording; without the latter it stays open for human inspection.

## Evidence

Final Release build: **0 warnings, 0 errors** (`docs/design/m6/build.log`). Used the requested quit-before-build routine and offline build flags. A build attempted while the isolated selftest still owned the apphost encountered a sharing lock; allowed the selftest to finish, then rebuilt cleanly. No process was killed.

Final selftest: **exit 0**, `SELFTEST PASS`, production internal show-to-focus **2.667 / 1.605 / 1.089 ms**, all <100 ms. Raw log: `docs/design/m6/selftest.log`. The baseline/heavy measurement smoke and integrated carousel smoke passed. These are scripted internal focus samples, not physical hotkey-to-paint or selected-layout performance.

Two earlier full runs passed (internal samples 1.875/1.100/1.314 ms and 2.686/1.735/1.743 ms). One final-binary run hit an existing measurement-child hide/cleanup timeout; raw failure retained in `docs/design/m6/selftest-timeout.log`. Sent the isolated measurement instance its supported graceful quit command (exit 0); subsequent process inventory showed no remaining AntiQuickPad apphost. The unchanged final binary passed on the isolated retry with no overlapping UI launches. Sandbox SendInput denied endpoints are not physical latency evidence.

Backdrop capability reports, OS API string `Microsoft Windows 10.0.26340`, Avalonia `12.1.4.0`:

| Requested mode | ActualTransparencyLevel |
|---|---|
| none | None |
| mica | Mica |
| acrylic | AcrylicBlur |
| blur | Transparent |

Each mode opened, recorded its actual level and closed gracefully with exit 0. `docs/design/m6/probe-*.txt` are the raw reports. This records the platform's reported level, not a visual endorsement of the compositor material.

## Visual QA and limits

- Repaired Card's clipped final text by revising vertical padding and reducing body to 16 DIP. The final main/bars/menu frames show the complete fixture including strike and last paragraph.
- Direct 144-DPI RTB rendering double-scaled nested card text and omitted menu/control children. Rejected those frames. Final renderer uses a fixed 960×800-DIP tree inside a Viewbox, explicitly scales the vector tree to 1×/1.5× and rasterizes via RTB at 96 DPI. This is new vector rasterization, not bitmap upsampling. Both scales now show the complete menu and consistent layout.
- Caret is real, shown after a render invalidation flush, at line 13 / column 21 in the indented task text. Empty states use a real empty document with a hint overlay; status reports line 1 / column 1 and zero words in Pro.
- Native Windows title bar/DWM shadow/corners and compositor backdrops cannot be captured by the client-tree RTB. Internal Card shadow/radii are actual opaque Avalonia Borders. No simulated desktop/backdrop is painted.
- Natural editor leading and flush-left code-panel text remain. Custom chevrons replace the harsh stock boxes without changing the installed folding manager. Offset-only scrolling/redraw, folding interaction and pointer target feel need hands-on validation before production integration.
- Main shows subdued bar opacity; bars shows full opacity and a More hover treatment. Hover-reveal and motion are proposals, intentionally not implemented in this design-selection task.
- Carousel frames are deterministic intermediate translations of two real editors; resting Card neighbour peeks are edge surfaces. They do not measure live animation quality. Card's dot range beyond eight notes still needs a production policy.
- The design host is a fixture/review mode, not a replacement resident shell: it never opens real notes or registers hotkeys/tray. More/pin/minimize/hide are real controls; New/Delete/navigation/menu-command layouts, rail search, and Settings are design affordances awaiting selected-direction integration. The in-tree menu is deliberate so RTB includes it; native popup behavior is not being claimed.

## Needs the user's eyes

1. Choose Quiet, Card or Pro by reviewing main **and** bars/menu/empty at both scales. Judge hierarchy, body weight, marker contrast and whether controls feel deliberate.
2. Quiet: subdued idle controls and writing inset. Card: available writing area, neighbour peeks and floating pager. Pro: density, rail usefulness and status/command-hint noise.
3. Open each live backdrop probe over light/dark content; inspect focus/deactivation, moving/resizing, text edges and monitor DPI changes. Mica/Acrylic were reported as granted; Blur was not.
4. After selection/integration: physical wheel/drag, checkbox/fold hit targets, focus-revealed bars, physical hotkey-to-paint and motion/reduced-motion behavior. Current performance evidence is bounded to scripted production-resident checks, not future animation or the new layouts integrated with persistence.

No git commands, network calls, STATUS.md/HANDOFF edits, or unrelated source changes. Manager owns git and selection follow-through. Per-direction checkpoints are in `.coord/status-T011.md`.


# T-012 � Core / ViewModels / Views refactor

Status: COMPLETE � final Release build, Core smoke, full selftest and all retained CLI entry points passed. Manager owns git. No git commands, network calls, new packages, process killing, shared STATUS/HANDOFF edits, or out-of-scope writes.

## Result

Package-free net10.0 Core owns notes, atomic persistence, settings and pure Markdown/text rules. UI ViewModels own note projections, commands, save/pin state and the settings adapter. The existing resident visual tree is now Views/Classic/ClassicShellWindow, implementing IShellView; Design shares its smaller IEditorHost contract. App constructs Core -> VM -> selected shell and injected platform services by hand. Only Classic is selectable today; Quiet/Card remain T-013. Unknown shell IDs fall back to Classic while retaining their stored identity.

The editor remains resident, its undo stack is cleared only on note binding, and text operations apply small Replace edits inside undo groups. Per-document VM routing avoids a static global edit callback. The UI scanner adapter maps Core metadata to Avalonia folding/rendering types. Carousel retains its original gate/drag/snap/preview behavior and selects through MainViewModel. The 350 ms dispatcher debounce, runtime scroll offsets and focus/animation stay UI-side.

Core has no UI package, project or assembly reference. Main/Note/Status VMs have no Avalonia dependency. Views do not open note/settings stores. Platform services own native hotkey/singleton/tray handling and window operation adapters; views and VMs never call Win32 directly. Classic still reads its own Avalonia visibility/normal-window state for focus and bounds capture; this is UI state, not backend logic.

